<section id="features">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="section-heading reveal">
            <span class="kicker">Features</span>
            <h2>Built around running several agents at once.</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="features-grid">
            <div class="feature-card win-card reveal">
                <div class="win-titlebar"><span class="win-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="win-filename">agents.txt</span></div>
                <div class="win-body">
                    <h3><span class="status-dot working"></span>An agent per worktree</h3>
                    <p>Each agent runs in its own git worktree on one repo and branch, so parallel work never collides. Start them from the dashboard or let Claude do it. Harness: Claude Code, or Neovim with Claude beside it.</p>
                    <div class="feature-visual mono">
                        <div>api · feat/pagination <span class="accent">claude</span></div>
                        <div>web · feat/pagination <span class="accent-magenta">nvim</span></div>
                        <div>remove <span class="accent">worktree optional · branch always kept</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="feature-card win-card reveal">
                <div class="win-titlebar"><span class="win-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="win-filename">notices.log</span></div>
                <div class="win-body">
                    <h3>Knows when an agent needs you</h3>
                    <p>A colored dot per agent shows what it's doing. When one asks a question, waits on a permission, is ready for review, fails, stalls, or its branch falls behind or conflicts, you get a desktop toast and a notice in the notification center. Alerts come from the projects in this window, remote ones included; the rest stay quiet in their own dashboard. Resolved and dismissed notices move to the center's History tab for a day.</p>
                    <div class="feature-visual mono">
                        <div><span class="accent-yellow">?</span> agent-1 <span class="accent">asks a question</span></div>
                        <div><span class="accent-green">✓</span> agent-2 <span class="accent">ready for review</span></div>
                        <div>shop <span class="accent-yellow">● 2</span> <span class="accent">open notices in this window</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="feature-card win-card reveal">
                <div class="win-titlebar"><span class="win-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="win-filename">dashboard.txt</span></div>
                <div class="win-body">
                    <h3>One dashboard per project</h3>
                    <p>Four tabs: Agents, Subs, Repositories and Notifications. See every agent's branch, harness and live status, and hide, stop or remove it from one place.</p>
                    <div class="feature-visual mono">
                        <div><span class="accent">Agents</span> · Subs · Repositories · Notifications</div>
                        <div>api · feat/pagination <span class="accent-green">✓ done</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="feature-card win-card reveal">
                <div class="win-titlebar"><span class="win-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="win-filename">repos.txt</span></div>
                <div class="win-body">
                    <h3>Repository management</h3>
                    <p>A project is a folder of repos. Add one (create or clone), pull, or change its default branch from the Repositories tab. Pulls never create merge commits.</p>
                    <div class="feature-visual mono">
                        <div>Repositories <span class="accent">tab</span></div>
                        <div>pull <span class="accent">fetch --prune · merge --ff-only</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="feature-card win-card reveal">
                <div class="win-titlebar"><span class="win-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="win-filename">iso.txt</span></div>
                <div class="win-body">
                    <h3>ISO mode</h3>
                    <p>For a machine whose data must not leave it. Others see only codes and states, there is no outbound ssh, update check or port forward, and agents are denied <code>git push</code> and PR create or merge. Enforced by fleet, not a sandbox: model API traffic still leaves.</p>
                    <div class="feature-visual mono">
                        <div>sub1.agent2: <span class="accent-yellow">waiting for input</span></div>
                        <div>git push <span class="accent-magenta">denied</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="feature-card win-card reveal">
                <div class="win-titlebar"><span class="win-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="win-filename">models.txt</span></div>
                <div class="win-body">
                    <h3>Auto model routing</h3>
                    <p>Set model and effort per role: head, main, sub, repo agent. Or set <code>auto</code> and Haiku picks the model and effort for each new sub or agent, falling back to your default if it times out.</p>
                    <div class="feature-visual mono">
                        <div>head · main · sub · agent <span class="accent">model + effort</span></div>
                        <div>auto <span class="accent">Haiku picks per new sub or agent</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="feature-card win-card reveal">
                <div class="win-titlebar"><span class="win-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="win-filename">todo.txt</span></div>
                <div class="win-body">
                    <h3>Todo board</h3>
                    <p>Type <code>+idea</code> in the main pane, run <code>fleet todo "idea"</code>, or let Claude file one. The board shows effort and status and launches a ticket as a sub-orchestrator. Jira pull is experimental: Jira Cloud only, read-only, not yet tested against a real Jira instance.</p>
                    <div class="feature-visual mono">
                        <div>&gt; <span class="accent-magenta">+cache the product list</span></div>
                        <div>t <span class="accent">board</span> · enter <span class="accent">open in nvim</span> · l <span class="accent">launch as sub</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="feature-card win-card reveal">
                <div class="win-titlebar"><span class="win-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="win-filename">stacks.txt</span></div>
                <div class="win-body">
                    <h3>Ports, stacks and secrets</h3>
                    <p>Start a project's dev stack on a remote, wait for its port and health check, and get it forwarded to your browser. Untracked files such as <code>.env</code> are copied into every new worktree; fleet never reads them.</p>
                    <div class="feature-visual mono">
                        <div>start_stack <span class="accent">run · wait for port · forward</span></div>
                        <div>localhost:5173 <span class="accent-green">← remote</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="feature-card win-card reveal">
                <div class="win-titlebar"><span class="win-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="win-filename">theme.txt</span></div>
                <div class="win-body">
                    <h3>15 themes, one look</h3>
                    <p><code>fleet theme set</code> restyles fleet, its Neovim, Claude Code and yazi live. On Omarchy, fleet follows every theme switch.</p>
                    <div class="feature-visual mono">
                        <div>$ fleet theme set <span class="accent-magenta">catppuccin-mocha</span></div>
                        <div>$ fleet theme install <span class="accent">omarchy</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <p class="install-note section-note reveal">Also included: one keybind model for fleet, its Neovim and Claude Code, with an in-app editor; <code>fleet update</code> with checksum verification; <code>fleet doctor</code>.</p>
    </div>
</section>
